items=[{'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-001', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter and his family live in a sand-bank under a fir-tree. His mother warns him not to enter Mr McGregor’s garden. Peter squeezes under the garden gate anyway.\n\nWhich detail marks Peter’s move into the forbidden setting?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter and his family live in a sand-bank under a fir-tree. His mother warns him not to enter Mr McGregor’s garden. Peter squeezes under the garden gate anyway.\n\nWhich detail marks Peter’s move into the forbidden setting?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'He squeezes under the garden gate.', 'is_correct': True}, {'text': 'He lives beneath a fir-tree.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'His mother speaks to him.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'His mother stays at home.', 'is_correct': False}], 'correct_index': 0, 'explanation': {'summary': 'The gate separates the garden from the land outside; crossing it takes Peter into the place his mother warned him about.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-002', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nTheir mother has warned them not to enter Mr McGregor’s garden. Peter’s siblings go down the lane to gather blackberries. Peter goes into Mr McGregor’s garden and eats vegetables.\n\nHow do the characters’ choices lead them into different situations?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nTheir mother has warned them not to enter Mr McGregor’s garden. Peter’s siblings go down the lane to gather blackberries. Peter goes into Mr McGregor’s garden and eats vegetables.\n\nHow do the characters’ choices lead them into different situations?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'They all follow the same path into the garden.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The siblings use the lane; Peter enters the garden his mother has forbidden.', 'is_correct': True}, {'text': 'Peter stays home while the siblings visit Mr McGregor.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The siblings chase Peter away from the berries.', 'is_correct': False}], 'correct_index': 1, 'explanation': {'summary': 'Their different choices of place separate the safe berry-gathering trip from Peter’s risky visit.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-003', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nWhile escaping through the garden, Peter’s jacket buttons catch in a gooseberry net. He frees himself by slipping out of the jacket.\n\nHow do the setting and Peter’s clothing combine to affect the event?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nWhile escaping through the garden, Peter’s jacket buttons catch in a gooseberry net. He frees himself by slipping out of the jacket.\n\nHow do the setting and Peter’s clothing combine to affect the event?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'The jacket opens a locked garden door.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The net helps him find the gate.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The garden net catches his buttons and briefly traps him.', 'is_correct': True}, {'text': 'The buttons frighten Mr McGregor away.', 'is_correct': False}], 'correct_index': 2, 'explanation': {'summary': 'The net is a feature of this setting, and the buttons catch in it. The obstacle changes Peter’s escape.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-004', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter hides inside a watering can in the tool-shed. The can contains water. When Peter sneezes, Mr McGregor hears him and rushes towards him.\n\nWhich event changes the hiding place from secret to discovered?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter hides inside a watering can in the tool-shed. The can contains water. When Peter sneezes, Mr McGregor hears him and rushes towards him.\n\nWhich event changes the hiding place from secret to discovered?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'Peter sees his own family.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'A mouse opens the tool-shed.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The watering can rolls into the garden.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'Mr McGregor hears Peter sneeze.', 'is_correct': True}], 'correct_index': 3, 'explanation': {'summary': 'The sound reveals Peter’s hiding place. The passage does not say that anyone sees him enter.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-005', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter asks a mouse the way to the gate. She has a large pea in her mouth and cannot answer. She shakes her head, and Peter begins to cry.\n\nWhich explanation fits this encounter without inventing the mouse’s feelings?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter asks a mouse the way to the gate. She has a large pea in her mouth and cannot answer. She shakes her head, and Peter begins to cry.\n\nWhich explanation fits this encounter without inventing the mouse’s feelings?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'She cannot speak because she is carrying a pea in her mouth.', 'is_correct': True}, {'text': 'She wants Peter to be caught.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'She has forgotten where her own home is.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'She is angry because Peter ate her food.', 'is_correct': False}], 'correct_index': 0, 'explanation': {'summary': 'The passage supplies a physical reason for her silence. It does not tell us that she dislikes Peter.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-006', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nMr McGregor has planted vegetables in his garden. Peter enters without permission and eats some. Mr McGregor chases him, calling him a thief.\n\nWhy does Mr McGregor describe Peter differently from a reader who feels sorry for him?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nMr McGregor has planted vegetables in his garden. Peter enters without permission and eats some. Mr McGregor chases him, calling him a thief.\n\nWhy does Mr McGregor describe Peter differently from a reader who feels sorry for him?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'He thinks Peter is lost at school.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'He sees Peter taking food from the garden he tends.', 'is_correct': True}, {'text': 'He invited Peter to collect the vegetables.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'He has not noticed that Peter is in the garden.', 'is_correct': False}], 'correct_index': 1, 'explanation': {'summary': 'From the gardener’s viewpoint, Peter is taking his food. This explains why he calls Peter a thief.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-007', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter climbs onto a wheelbarrow and sees the gate beyond Mr McGregor. Mr McGregor is facing away. Peter runs along a path and slips under the gate.\n\nWhich pair of setting details makes this escape possible?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nPeter climbs onto a wheelbarrow and sees the gate beyond Mr McGregor. Mr McGregor is facing away. Peter runs along a path and slips under the gate.\n\nWhich pair of setting details makes this escape possible?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'The locked door opens, and the mouse carries him.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The net becomes a bridge, and his jacket pulls him across.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The wheelbarrow gives him a view, and the gate gives him a way out.', 'is_correct': True}, {'text': 'The pond dries up, and the cat guides him.', 'is_correct': False}], 'correct_index': 2, 'explanation': {'summary': 'The raised wheelbarrow helps Peter locate the exit. The gap under the gate is the route he actually uses.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}, {'id': 'AC9E3LE01-P-008', 'subject': 'english', 'year_level': 'Year 3', 'curriculum_code': 'AC9E3LE01', 'bank': 'practice', 'stage': 'apply', 'skill': 'Characters, events and settings in context', 'question': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nAt home, Peter is tired and unwell, so his mother puts him to bed. His siblings eat the blackberries they gathered, with bread and milk.\n\nHow does the ending connect with the siblings’ earlier event?', 'audio_prompt': 'Based on The Tale of Peter Rabbit by Beatrix Potter. This short retelling supplies the details for the question.\n\nAt home, Peter is tired and unwell, so his mother puts him to bed. His siblings eat the blackberries they gathered, with bread and milk.\n\nHow does the ending connect with the siblings’ earlier event?', 'visual': {'type': 'none'}, 'answers': [{'text': 'They now enter the garden to search for Peter’s jacket.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'They give Mr McGregor the vegetables Peter ate.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'They become ill after hiding in the watering can.', 'is_correct': False}, {'text': 'The blackberries at supper are the food they gathered on their trip.', 'is_correct': True}], 'correct_index': 3, 'explanation': {'summary': 'The berries connect their earlier gathering with the ending. Peter’s return is different: he goes to bed instead of sharing their supper.', 'hint': 'Use only the supplied story details; distinguish what happens from what you imagine.'}, 'source': {'title': 'The Tale of Peter Rabbit', 'creator': 'Beatrix Potter', 'url': 'https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm', 'adaptation': 'Original short retelling of the public-domain story; source text read in full. Illustrations not reviewed.'}}]
SOURCES={
'dot':('Dot and the Kangaroo','Ethel C. Pedley','https://www.gutenberg.org/files/18891/18891-h/18891-h.htm','Selected scenes from Chapters I–II; original short retelling of public-domain prose.'),
'alice':('Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland','Lewis Carroll','https://www.gutenberg.org/files/11/11-h/11-h.htm','Selected scenes from Chapters I–II; original short retelling of public-domain prose.'),
'aesop':('The Fables of Aesop','Aesopic tradition, retold by Joseph Jacobs','https://www.gutenberg.org/files/28/28-h/28-h.htm','Selected named fables read; original short retellings of the public-domain 1894 text.'),
'wombat':('Wombat and Mother Earth','Helen Milroy (Palyku)','https://fremantlepress.com.au/2019/05/22/three-free-extracts-from-fremantle-press-titles-to-use-in-your-classroom-this-naidoc-week/','Complete publisher-authorised story extract read. Brief scene summaries for analysis, not a reproduction of the story.'),
'peter':('The Tale of Peter Rabbit','Beatrix Potter','https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14838/14838-h/14838-h.htm','Original short retelling of public-domain prose; text read in full.')}
def base(bank,key,text,ask,scene=''):
 title,creator,url,scope=SOURCES[key]
 credit=f'{title} — {creator}.'
 if scene:credit+=' '+scene+'.'
 q=credit+'\nShort scene summary: '+text+'\n\n'+ask
 n=sum(x['bank']==bank for x in items)+1
 return dict(id=f'AC9E3LE01-{bank[0].upper()}-{n:03}',subject='english',year_level='Year 3',curriculum_code='AC9E3LE01',bank=bank,stage='apply',skill='Discuss characters, events and settings in context',question=q,audio_prompt=q,visual={'type':'none'},source={'title':title,'creator':creator,'url':url,'adaptation':scope,'scene':scene})
def mc(bank,key,text,ask,correct,wrong,why,scene=''):
 x=base(bank,key,text,ask,scene);p=sum(a['bank']==bank and a.get('grading_mode')!='adult-review' for a in items)%4
 opts=list(wrong);opts.insert(p,correct)
 x.update(answers=[{'text':v,'is_correct':i==p} for i,v in enumerate(opts)],correct_index=p,explanation={'summary':why,'hint':'Connect the action to the supplied story details.'});items.append(x)
def task(bank,key,text,ask,model,note,scene=''):
 x=base(bank,key,text,ask,scene)
 x.update(grading_mode='adult-review',answers=[],correct_index=None,model_answer=model,acceptance_note=note,response_instructions='Discuss your response with an adult, or write it for an adult to check. Explain your idea using the supplied details.',completion_label='I have discussed or written my response; an adult still needs to check it.',explanation={'summary':'An adult checks your explanation against the scene and the task.','hint':'Give a reason from the supplied details; more than one interpretation may fit.'});items.append(x)
# Australian literary bush fantasy: different obstacles, dialogue and relationships.
mc('practice','dot','Dot is allowed to gather flowers near her cottage. She follows a hare into the bush. When she stops, she cannot see the cottage.','Which event first takes Dot beyond the familiar setting?','She follows the hare away from the cottage.',['She asks to pick flowers near home.','She stops to look for the cottage.','She remembers her mother’s instruction.'],'Following the hare changes her location; looking for the cottage happens afterwards.','Chapter I')
mc('practice','dot','Dot is lost and upset. A Kangaroo stays beside her, with tears on her own face. The Kangaroo has also lost someone: her baby.','What connects the two characters’ experiences?','Both are upset because they are separated from someone or something important.',['Both are searching for the same cottage.','Both have lost their own babies.','Both are trying to catch a hare.'],'The losses differ: Dot is away from home and the Kangaroo misses her baby. The shared experience is separation.','Chapter I')
mc('practice','dot','In this fantasy, berries let Dot understand animal speech. Ants, lizards and birds were already around her, but now their voices become clear.','What changes after the magical event?','Dot’s understanding of the creatures changes.',['The bush turns into a schoolroom.','The animals appear for the first time.','Every creature becomes a human child.'],'The creatures were present already; the new ability changes Dot’s experience of the same place.','Chapter I')
mc('practice','dot','The nearest water-hole is far away. Dot’s tired legs give way after a few steps. The Kangaroo offers to carry her in her pouch.','How does the solution fit both the character and the setting?','The Kangaroo can carry Dot over the long journey she cannot walk.',['The pouch brings the water-hole closer.','Dot’s legs become stronger immediately.','The Kangaroo moves Dot’s cottage beside them.'],'The distance remains, but the Kangaroo supplies a way for the exhausted child to travel.','Chapter I')
task('practice','dot','Dot says she has lost her way. The Kangaroo asks what this “way” looks like so she can find it. Dot explains what she means.','Explain the misunderstanding. Why does Dot need to explain instead of simply repeating the same words?','The Kangaroo treats a way like a missing object. Dot means she does not know the route home, so she must explain that meaning.','Require the difference between an object and a route, linked to this conversation.','Chapter I')
task('practice','dot','The Kangaroo looks at Dot’s torn dress and compares it with her own fur, which has survived the journey. She thinks a kangaroo is better suited to the bush.','Explain why the Kangaroo judges the clothing this way. Would her judgement necessarily fit a character living indoors?','She uses moving through scrub as her test, and her fur has coped better. Indoors, the same torn-clothing problem might not happen.','Require bush evidence and a clearly hypothetical contrast; do not demand agreement that one creature is generally superior.','Chapter I')
task('practice','dot','Tall trees and tangled leaves hide much of the sky. The light is fading. Dot worries about being alone in the bush after dark.','Explain how two features of this setting make Dot’s problem harder.','The trees limit what she can see, and fading light means she has less daylight left to find her way.','Require two supported features and their connection to being lost.','Chapter I')
task('practice','dot','Dot is unhappy under a tree. Later, the Kangaroo carries her through the scrub. Dot feels gently rocked and begins to sing.','Discuss the change in Dot’s experience of travelling. What has changed besides the scenery?','Earlier she was unhappy. Now she has a helpful companion and a gently rocking way to travel, and she begins singing.','Accept supported explanations of companionship, support or comfort; do not say she has already reached home.','Chapter I')
task('practice','dot','The Kangaroo offers Dot her pouch for the trip to water. Once Dot is settled, the Kangaroo says it feels as though her missing baby is with her again.','Explain how this action helps both characters in different ways.','Dot gets transport, while carrying someone gives the Kangaroo comfort because she misses her baby.','Require the practical benefit to Dot and the stated emotional connection for the Kangaroo.','Chapter I')
task('practice','dot','Dot finds the rocky descent frightening. The Kangaroo says she knows every jump and chooses it instead of a route through thick trees.','Discuss why the same route can seem dangerous to Dot but manageable to the Kangaroo.','The descent frightens Dot, while the Kangaroo knows the jumps and sees the thick trees on the other route as obstacles.','Require different knowledge or movement abilities with evidence; familiarity does not mean every real cliff is safe.','Chapter II')
# World literary fantasy: curiosity, physical scale, problem changes, and narrative expectation.
mc('practice','alice','Alice is sitting beside her sister. A rabbit takes a watch from a waistcoat pocket. Alice has never seen a rabbit do this and follows it.','Which detail gives Alice a reason to investigate?','The rabbit has a pocket and uses a watch.',['Her sister continues reading beside her.','The field has ordinary daisies in it.','The day is warm enough to feel sleepy.'],'The unusual rabbit behaviour sparks Alice’s curiosity.','Chapter I')
mc('practice','alice','Alice enters a rabbit-hole. The passage suddenly drops steeply, and she falls before she can stop.','How does the setting change her control of events?','The sudden drop turns following the rabbit into an unexpected fall.',['The level path lets her stop whenever she chooses.','The hole leads her straight back to her sister.','The rabbit carries her down a staircase.'],'The abrupt change in the tunnel causes the fall.','Chapter I')
mc('practice','alice','A tiny key does not fit the large doors in the hall. Alice later finds a small door behind a curtain, and the key opens it.','Why is discovering the hidden door important?','It gives the previously unhelpful key a use.',['It proves that all the large doors were open.','It makes the key disappear from the story.','It brings Alice back above ground immediately.'],'The key can work only after Alice finds a matching lock.','Chapter I')
mc('practice','alice','Alice shrinks until she can fit through a small doorway. But she has left its key on a high glass table, and she can no longer reach it.','What has happened to her problem?','Solving the size problem has created a new problem reaching the key.',['Every obstacle has disappeared at once.','The table has changed into a rabbit.','The doorway has become too small again.'],'Her smaller size helps with the doorway but prevents her reaching the key.','Chapter I')
task('practice','alice','As Alice falls slowly, she notices shelves on the walls. She takes an empty jar, then puts it into a cupboard rather than dropping it on anyone below.','What does this small action suggest about Alice even during a surprising event? Support your idea.','She is considerate because she avoids dropping something that could hurt someone beneath her.','Accept care, thoughtfulness or similar with the jar action; do not claim a person actually stands below.','Chapter I')
task('practice','alice','The hall is dark, but through a tiny doorway Alice sees bright flowers and fountains in a garden. She wants to reach the garden.','Explain how the contrast between the two settings helps the reader understand Alice’s goal.','The garden looks inviting compared with the dark hall, so the reader can understand why she wants to get through.','Require an actual contrast and its connection to her wish, not an invented event in the garden.','Chapter I')
task('practice','alice','Alice follows a strange rabbit without considering how to return. Later, in the hall, she tries doors and tests a key to find a way out.','Compare how Alice acts at these two points.','At first she acts immediately out of curiosity. In the hall she tests possible exits, although she still faces problems.','Require a contrast between the supplied actions; accept wording such as impulsive and searching without requiring these labels.','Chapter I')
task('practice','alice','After many strange events, Alice eats a little cake and expects her size to change. Nothing happens at first, which she finds surprising.','Why does an ordinary event surprise Alice in this story?','She has started expecting impossible changes, so staying the same size now feels unusual to her.','Require the connection between earlier strangeness and her changed expectations.','Chapter I')
# Aesopic fables: specific episodes and genuinely different interpretive tasks.
mc('practice','aesop','A dog carries meat across a plank over a brook. He mistakes his reflection for another dog with food. When he snaps at it, his meat falls into the water.','Which feature of the setting makes his mistake possible?','The water reflects his image.',['The plank hides all of the water.','The brook contains a second real dog.','The meat changes into a pebble.'],'The apparent rival is a reflection in the brook.','The Dog and the Shadow')
mc('practice','aesop','A country mouse visits a town dining room with cakes and jellies. Two large dogs rush in, and both mice run away. The visitor decides to return home.','Which event changes the visitor’s decision about staying?','The dogs interrupt the meal and force an escape.',['The town mouse offers a fine meal.','The cousins finish a long journey.','The country mouse sees the cakes.'],'The danger, rather than the attractive food, causes the visitor to leave.','The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse')
mc('practice','aesop','A fox wants a crow’s cheese. He praises her and asks her to sing. When she opens her beak, the cheese drops and the fox takes it.','How do the fox’s words help him get what he wants?','They persuade the crow to open the beak holding the cheese.',['They make the tree bend to the ground.','They persuade the crow to hide the cheese.','They call another fox to climb the tree.'],'The praise leads to an action that releases the cheese.','The Fox and the Crow')
mc('practice','aesop','A lion laughs at a mouse’s offer to help him someday but lets it go. Later, the mouse gnaws through ropes that hold the lion.','What challenges the lion’s earlier expectation?','The small mouse can do something useful for the large lion.',['The lion never needs any help.','The ropes prevent the mouse from approaching.','The mouse becomes larger than the lion.'],'The mouse’s ability matters more than its size in this problem.','The Lion and the Mouse')
mc('practice','aesop','A tall tree boasts that no wind can bend it. A storm uproots it, but a small reed bends and later stands upright again.','How does the weather test the characters’ claims?','The storm shows that bending can help the reed survive.',['The storm proves that the tree cannot be moved.','The reed defeats the storm by growing into a tree.','The weather leaves both exactly as before.'],'Their different responses to the same storm challenge the tree’s boast.','The Tree and the Reed')
mc('practice','aesop','A crow finds water too low in a pitcher to reach. It drops pebbles in one at a time until the water rises within reach.','Which change solves the crow’s difficulty?','The water rises after the pebbles are added.',['The crow’s beak grows longer.','The pitcher becomes a shallow plate.','Another bird pours in more water.'],'The solution changes the water level, not the crow’s body.','The Crow and the Pitcher')
mc('practice','aesop','The Wind and Sun compete to make a traveller remove a cloak. Strong wind makes the traveller wrap it tighter. Warm sunshine makes the traveller take it off.','Why do the two attempts have different results?','The traveller responds differently to strong wind and warmth.',['The traveller forgets to bring a cloak for the Sun.','The Wind secretly gives the Sun a new cloak.','The two attempts happen to different travellers.'],'The same person protects against wind but removes the cloak when warm.','The Wind and the Sun')
mc('practice','aesop','Mice suggest putting a bell on a cat to warn them when it comes near. They like the plan until an older mouse asks who will attach the bell. Nobody answers.','What problem does the older mouse reveal?','The plan lacks someone able to carry out its dangerous first step.',['The bell would make no sound at all.','The cat has already promised to help.','The mice have decided they want the cat nearby.'],'A useful warning is not enough if no one can safely attach it.','Belling the Cat')
task('practice','aesop','A mother crab tells her child to walk straight instead of sideways. The young crab asks her to demonstrate so it can follow.','How does the child’s reply change the conversation from advice to a test of the adviser?','The child asks the mother to show the action herself, so her advice must now be demonstrated rather than only spoken.','Require the request for a demonstration; do not invent a later successful demonstration.','The Two Crabs')
task('practice','aesop','A father asks his sons to break a tied bundle of sticks. None can. When the bundle is untied, each son can break a single stick.','Explain how the objects help the father communicate an idea about people working together.','The bundle stays strong while separate sticks break. It gives the sons a concrete example of strength from staying united.','Require both parts of the demonstration and a reasonable connection to cooperation.','The Bundle of Sticks')
task('practice','aesop','A dog offers a hungry wolf regular food at its master’s home. The wolf notices a worn patch on the dog’s neck and learns that the dog is chained at night. The wolf leaves.','Discuss which new detail changes the wolf’s view of the offer. What does its choice suggest it values?','The chain changes the offer because the food comes with a restriction. Leaving suggests the wolf values freedom despite being hungry.','Require the new restriction and its connection to the decision; do not turn the story’s choice into advice to refuse needed care.','The Dog and the Wolf')
task('practice','aesop','A hare races ahead, then stops to nap. A tortoise keeps moving and reaches the finish before the hare can catch up.','Explain why predicting the winner from speed alone would miss an important part of this story.','The hare is faster but stops. The tortoise keeps using its time, so actions during the race change the result.','Require speed contrasted with the nap and continued movement.','The Hare and the Tortoise')
task('practice','aesop','A brass pot and an earthenware pot float down a river. The brass pot promises not to strike the other. The earthenware pot still stays away, fearing an accidental collision could damage it.','Explain why a friendly promise does not remove this character’s concern.','The river can bring them together accidentally, and the more fragile pot could break even without anyone intending harm.','Require the difference between intention and accidental contact; do not say the pots actually collide.','The Two Pots')
task('practice','aesop','At home, the country mouse offers a simple meal without interruption. In town, fine food is interrupted by dogs, and the visitor runs away.','Discuss whether the grander dining room is necessarily the better place for this visitor. Use a detail about each setting.','It has finer food, but the country setting offers a peaceful meal. For this visitor the danger outweighs the richer food.','Require a two-setting comparison, not a claim that every town is unsafe.','The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse')
task('practice','aesop','The dog loses its meat after snapping at the reflected image of a dog with meat. The crow gets water by adding pebbles to a pitcher.','Compare how these two characters respond to something they cannot immediately obtain.','The dog acts on a mistaken appearance and loses its food. The crow changes the physical problem gradually and gets the water.','Require a difference in actions and outcomes using both supplied scenes.','The Dog and the Shadow; The Crow and the Pitcher')
task('practice','aesop','A fox praises a crow to make her drop cheese. A mouse frees a lion by gnawing its ropes after the lion once spared it.','Compare what each helper-looking action is really doing for the other character.','The fox’s praise benefits the fox at the crow’s expense. The mouse’s action really helps the lion escape.','Require the contrast between self-interested persuasion and practical help; do not insist every compliment is dishonest.','The Fox and the Crow; The Lion and the Mouse')
task('practice','aesop','A tree boasts before a storm uproots it; a bending reed survives. A hare boasts before a race, then loses after napping.','What similar gap appears between these characters’ words and the later events? Explain the different causes.','Both boast but do not get the expected result. The tree is uprooted by a storm, while the hare loses because it stops racing.','Require the common gap and the different event causing it; avoid a generic one-word moral.','The Tree and the Reed; The Hare and the Tortoise')
task('practice','aesop','The mice applaud a plan to bell their cat but cannot say who will do it. The crow actually drops pebbles into a pitcher until water rises.','Discuss the difference between having a promising idea and reaching a solution in these scenes.','The mice still lack a way to carry out their idea. The crow performs actions that change the problem.','Require the unperformed step versus the performed sequence; do not invent the cat being belled.','Belling the Cat; The Crow and the Pitcher')
task('practice','aesop','A traveller grips a cloak tightly in strong wind, then removes it in warm sunshine. A reed bends during a storm, then stands again.','Compare how the characters respond to the weather. Describe an action and its result in each scene.','The traveller adjusts the cloak to conditions. The reed bends with the wind and survives, so both responses depend on the weather.','Require one supported action-result link per scene, without claiming the reed makes a human plan.','The Wind and the Sun; The Tree and the Reed')
# Four bounded analyses of an authentic contemporary First Nations literary work.
# Scene summaries + asks + models total <200 words, with no quotations.
task('practice','wombat','Mother Earth feels lonely. Wombat listens and shares stories.','How does his response address her need?','His company answers loneliness.','Require a supported connection.','Selected scene')
task('practice','wombat','Wombat becomes cold while comforting Mother Earth. She offers an underground shelter.','Explain how their helping roles change.','The helper now receives help.','Require the change in roles.','Selected scene')
task('practice','wombat','Wombat once slept above ground. Mother Earth shelters him below, away from the morning sun.','How does the new setting change his rest?','His new resting place is sheltered from the morning sun.','Require a setting–event connection.','Selected scene')
# Test: new scenes/questions, including four fables not used in Practice.
mc('test','dot','As they approach water, the Kangaroo asks Dot to stop singing. She moves quietly, sniffing and listening before each leap.','What do these changes in action suggest about this part of the setting?','It calls for caution and attention to possible danger.',['It is where the Kangaroo wants to hold a concert.','It has become Dot’s familiar bedroom.','It contains no sounds or smells at all.'],'Stopping the song and checking the surroundings show caution.','Chapter II')
mc('test','dot','The Kangaroo asks Dot to wait behind the bushes while birds drink. She explains that seeing a human would frighten them away from the water.','Why must Dot stay out of sight during this event?','Her presence could stop the thirsty birds coming down.',['She needs to teach the birds a song first.','The birds have already finished drinking.','The bushes contain her lost cottage.'],'The request is linked to the birds’ fear of humans, not to Dot wanting to harm them.','Chapter II')
task('test','dot','At the rocky descent, Dot notices a stone polished by generations of kangaroos passing on their way to water. The Kangaroo connects the mark with their long history.','Explain why this feature is more than an obstacle in their path.','The worn stone also records earlier journeys, connecting this trip with many kangaroos before them.','Require the connection between the physical mark and repeated journeys; no invented cultural attribution.','Chapter II')
task('test','dot','Thirsty birds wait near the water because they are afraid to approach it. The Kangaroo listens, sniffs and goes nearer to check the area for them.','Discuss what the Kangaroo’s actions reveal about her role in this scene.','She acts as a cautious helper, checking a place the birds fear before they approach.','Require both help and caution with an action as evidence; do not say she proves there can never be danger.','Chapter II')
mc('test','alice','After growing very tall, Alice can pick up the key. But she can only peer through the tiny doorway with one eye.','Which description explains why her goal is still out of reach?','She has the key but is now too large to enter.',['She has become too small to see the key.','The garden has turned into the hall.','The door opens straight onto her home.'],'The new size solves reaching the key but prevents passage through the doorway.','Chapter II')
mc('test','alice','Alice reaches the bottom of the strange fall on a heap of sticks and dry leaves. She is unhurt, stands up and sees the rabbit ahead.','Which event allows the pursuit to continue immediately?','She lands unhurt and can stand up again.',['The rabbit returns to carry her home.','Her sister opens the hall door.','The shelves disappear into the garden.'],'Her safe landing and ability to move allow her to follow; the other events are not supplied.','Chapter I')
mc('test','aesop','A fox serves soup on a shallow plate that suits him but not a stork’s long beak. On her return invitation, the stork serves food in a narrow-necked jar the fox cannot reach into.','What creates the reversal between the two meals?','The serving container now suits the stork instead of the fox.',['The fox and stork exchange bodies.','Both meals are served in the same dish.','The stork forgets to provide any food.'],'The container changes which guest can eat.','The Fox and the Stork')
mc('test','aesop','A cock finds a pearl in a farmyard but says a grain of barley would be more useful to him.','Why does the character judge the discovery differently from someone seeking jewellery?','He values food he can use more than an ornament.',['He cannot see anything shining in the straw.','He believes a pearl is already a grain.','He is searching for a lost necklace to wear.'],'The character’s needs shape the value he gives the object.','The Cock and the Pearl')
mc('test','aesop','In a theatre storeroom, a fox is frightened by what looks like a face. Looking closely, he discovers an actor’s mask.','What changes the fox’s understanding of the scene?','Closer inspection shows the face is an object used in plays.',['The mask starts talking and walking.','The theatre becomes a forest.','An actor tells the fox to hide.'],'The new observation explains the frightening appearance.','The Fox and the Mask')
mc('test','aesop','A dog sleeps in an ox’s feeding place. When the ox comes for straw, the dog barks and blocks its approach. The ox goes away without eating.','How does the dog’s choice affect the other character?','It prevents the ox from using its food supply.',['It helps the ox finish its meal faster.','It turns the straw into food for dogs.','It gives the ox a new feeding place.'],'Blocking access has a practical consequence for the ox.','The Dog in the Manger')
task('test','aesop','At one dinner the fox can eat from a shallow dish while the stork cannot. At another the stork can eat from a narrow jar while the fox cannot.','Compare the two hosts’ treatment of their guests. What shared problem do the objects create?','Each host uses a container that excludes the guest. Different objects create the same unequal access to food.','Require the pattern across both scenes; the learner need not approve revenge.','The Fox and the Stork')
task('test','aesop','A cock rejects a pearl because it cannot feed him. A hungry ox is kept from straw by a dog lying in its feeding place.','Compare how food needs drive these two scenes even though the characters face different obstacles.','The cock finds something that is not useful food. The ox has useful food nearby but cannot reach it because of the dog.','Require the different obstacles and a food-related need for each character.','The Cock and the Pearl; The Dog in the Manger')
task('test','aesop','The fox initially fears a mask but changes his view after examining it. In Beatrix Potter’s Peter Rabbit, Peter sees a cat watching fish and chooses not to speak to it after recalling a warning about cats.','Compare how these characters use information before deciding what to do.','The fox relies on a closer look at the object. Peter uses a remembered warning when deciding to avoid the cat.','Require the contrast in sources of information; do not claim the cat actually attacks Peter.','The Fox and the Mask; Peter Rabbit comparison')
items[-1]['source']['comparison_source']=SOURCES['peter'][2]
task('test','aesop','In The Fox and the Mask, a frightening face turns out to be a theatre object. In Lewis Carroll’s Alice, a rabbit using a pocket watch leads Alice to follow it.','Compare how an unusual sight changes each character’s actions.','The fox investigates and discovers an ordinary object; Alice follows because the rabbit’s behaviour is extraordinary.','Require an action and reason from each scene; do not label every strange appearance dangerous.','The Fox and the Mask; Alice comparison')
items[-1]['source']['comparison_source']=SOURCES['alice'][2]
task('test','peter','A cat watches fish in a pond and moves only its tail. Peter remembers his cousin’s warning about cats and passes quietly without speaking.','What does this scene show about Peter’s decision, and what does it leave unknown about the cat?','Peter chooses caution because of the warning. The scene does not tell us whether the cat would have chased him.','Require the reason for Peter’s choice and a distinction between possible and stated events.','Selected pond scene')
task('test','wombat','Mother Earth protects Wombat’s rest; his dreams renew her warmth.','Discuss how the ending connects their wellbeing.','Each benefits the other.','Require mutual benefit.','Selected ending')
